        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/7731a7d9-d8a4-4642-94d3-2cf21840dc04,1790355210659/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C67%2C1024%2C576%29%3B' alt='A band plays in the forecourt of a large stone building.' width='620' height='349' title='LONDON, ENGLAND - SEPTEMBER 11: Members from the Massed Bands of the Guards Division play on the forecourt of Buckingham Palace in London, during a commemorative Changing the Guard, in honour of the 25th anniversary of 9/11 terror attacks on September 11, 2026 in London, England. (Photo by Ben Whitley - WPA Pool/Getty Images)'/><p>While there have been efforts to emphasize that Buckingham Palace will continue to be the working and ceremonial headquarters of the monarchy, the recent confirmation that King Charles won\u2019t be living there could also be sending signals about the institution and its future.</p>",
